//? Title: Custom Gradients: Writing the Backward Pass Yourself
//? Subtitle: The contract a backward function must meet, numerically stable gradients, straight-through estimators and their bias, gradient reversal, and quantisation-aware training of ternary weights on mtcars, using datamunge's nn::Function
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Automatic differentiation supplies the derivative of every built-in operation. Sometimes a model
needs a different backward pass. A new operation may have no derivative in the library. The
composed derivative may overflow where a direct formula would not. Or the true derivative may be
useless, as it is for rounding, which is zero almost everywhere. This report states the
contract a custom backward must meet, a vector-Jacobian product, and shows how `nn::Function`
pairs a forward with a hand-written backward. A custom gradient for erfc matches its closed form to eight decimals and
passes first- and second-order checks. With its sign flipped it fails `gradcheck` by 2.26. A naively composed \(\log(1+e^x)\) returns
NaN gradients in float32 from \(x=89\), while a custom function and `nn::softplus` stay exact. The straight-through
estimator (STE) moves a rounded parameter to its target in 13 steps where the true gradient never moves it. The theory section derives its bias under stochastic
rounding. The mean STE gradient is \(2(x-t)\), while the expected loss has slope \(2(x-t)+1-2p\). A Monte Carlo
check confirms both to 0.014. Following the STE stops at \(x=t\), where the expected loss is 0.21, instead of at its minimum 0.09. On mtcars,
training ten ternary weights through the quantiser cuts the error of rounding the least-squares weights from 4.24 to 2.51 mpg at \(\Delta=1\),
against an exhaustive optimum of 2.33. At \(\Delta=2\) the quantised weights flip every step instead of converging.
The library gained `nn::straight_through`, `nn::round_ste`, `nn::stochastic_round_ste`, `nn::fake_quantize`,
`nn::gradient_reversal`, `nn::scale_gradient` and `nn::clip_gradient`.
)

\toc

> The problem

The reverse-mode sweep of the automatic differentiation report (`autodiff`, in this group) needs one backward
function per operation. Most of the time the library's own are right. Three situations call for
writing one by hand:

1. *A new operation.* An operation implemented outside the tape, such as a special function, a call into another
   library or a fused kernel, has no recorded history, so gradients cannot pass through it.
2. *A better formula.* The composed derivative of a correct forward can be numerically poor,
   for example when an intermediate overflows although the final value is moderate.
3. *A deliberate lie.* The derivative of rounding, sign or argmax is zero almost everywhere, so no
   gradient reaches the parameters before the operation. Training a quantised network or a
   discrete code needs a surrogate gradient, and adversarial objectives need a gradient with its sign reversed.

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.1_tensors_graphs_sessions/custom_gradient.cpp`,
a port of TensorFlow's custom-gradient example. TensorFlow looks up a gradient function per op type in a global registry
(`REGISTER_GRADIENT_OP`, or `tf.custom_gradient` in Python). In `nn` there is no registry: an
`nn::Function` carries its own backward, and `nn::apply_function` runs one without naming it.

> The method

>> The contract of a backward function

An operation \(y=\phi(x_1,\dots,x_k)\) with \(y\in\mathbb R^m\) and \(x_j\in\mathbb R^{n_j}\) takes part in the backward sweep only through
its vector-Jacobian products. Given the adjoint \(\bar y=\partial L/\partial y\) of its output, it must return, for each input,

$$
\bar x_j=\Bigl(\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial x_j}\Bigr)^{\!\top}\bar y\in\mathbb R^{n_j},
$$
\alttext(x bar j equals the transpose of the Jacobian of phi with respect to x j, times y bar, a vector in R n j.)

with the same shape as \(x_j\), or nothing for an input that needs no gradient. Nothing else is required. In
particular the backward never needs the full Jacobian, which for an elementwise operation is diagonal, so
\(\bar x=\phi'(x)\odot\bar y\). In `nn` a backward receives the inputs, the outputs and the output adjoints, and returns one
tensor per input:

```
nn::Function erfc_fn("Erfc",
  [](const Tensors& in) { return Tensors{nn::erfc(in[0])}; },                // forward
  [](const Tensors& in, const Tensors& out, const Tensors& g) {             // backward
    return Tensors{g[0] * nn::exp(-nn::square(in[0])) * (-2 / std::sqrt(pi))};
  });
```

The forward runs with recording switched off on detached inputs, and only the custom node is put on the
tape. If the backward is written with `nn` operations, those are recorded under `create_graph`, so the custom
gradient can itself be differentiated. A backward written with raw loops would give first derivatives only.

For \(\operatorname{erfc}(x)=\tfrac{2}{\sqrt\pi}\int_x^\infty e^{-s^2}\,\mathrm ds\), the fundamental theorem of calculus gives

$$
\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm dx}\operatorname{erfc}(x)=-\frac{2}{\sqrt\pi}e^{-x^2},\qquad
\frac{\mathrm d^2}{\mathrm dx^2}\operatorname{erfc}(x)=\frac{4x}{\sqrt\pi}e^{-x^2}.
$$
\alttext(The derivative of erfc of x is minus 2 over root pi times e to the minus x squared, and the second derivative is 4 x over root pi times e to the minus x squared.)

A wrong backward fails silently: training still runs, only worse. The check is the one from the previous
report. `nn::gradcheck` compares the backward with central differences of the forward in float64, and
`nn::gradgradcheck` does the same for the derivative of the backward.

>> Stable gradients

For softplus \(f(x)=\log(1+e^x)\), the composition of `exp`, `add` and `log` gives the derivative
\(\frac{1}{1+e^x}\cdot e^x\), evaluated as written. In float32, \(e^x\) overflows to \(\infty\) once \(x>\log(3.4\times10^{38})=88.72\). The
forward is then \(\infty\), and the backward is \(\infty/\infty\), which is NaN, although the true value is \(x\) and the true derivative
is 1. The algebraically equal forms

$$
f(x)=\max(x,0)+\log\bigl(1+e^{-\lvert x\rvert}\bigr),\qquad
f'(x)=\sigma(x)=\begin{cases}1/(1+e^{-x}),&x\ge0,\\e^{x}/(1+e^{x}),&x<0,\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(f of x equals max of x and 0 plus log of 1 plus e to the minus absolute x. Its derivative is the logistic sigma of x, evaluated as 1 over 1 plus e to the minus x for x at least 0, and as e to the x over 1 plus e to the x for x negative.)

only ever exponentiate non-positive numbers. A custom function can use one form for the value and the other for
the gradient. The same pattern stabilises log-sum-exp, norms near zero and the logit of a probability.

>> Straight-through estimators

Let \(q\) be a quantiser, for example \(q(x)=\Delta\,\mathrm{round}(x/\Delta)\). Its derivative is 0 except at the jumps, so in \(L(q(x))\) the
gradient \(L'(q)\,q'(x)\) is 0 and \(x\) never moves. The straight-through estimator (Hinton, 2012; Bengio, Léonard and Courville,
2013) uses the quantised value in the forward pass and replaces \(q'\) by 1 in the backward:

$$
\widehat{\nabla}_xL=L'\bigl(q(x)\bigr).
$$
\alttext(The straight-through gradient of L with respect to x is L prime evaluated at q of x.)

The identity \(y=x+\mathrm{stop}(q(x)-x)\) implements it with no custom function: its value is \(q(x)\), and its derivative is that of \(x\).
That is `nn::straight_through(x, value)`. `nn::round_ste` is the same as a custom node. `nn::fake_quantize` adds
the clipped variant used by TensorFlow's FakeQuant: values are clipped to the grid range \([\Delta q_{\min},\Delta q_{\max}]\), and the
gradient is zeroed outside that range, where the forward is constant.

An STE gradient is not the gradient of anything in general, so it carries no descent guarantee. Gradient descent on
\(x\) stops where \(L'(q(x))=0\). That is a point where the *quantised* value is stationary for \(L\), not a minimiser of \(L\circ q\).

>> The bias of the straight-through estimator

Stochastic rounding makes this precise. Take \(\Delta=1\) and \(q_s(x)=\lfloor x\rfloor+B\) with \(B\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(p)\) and \(p=x-\lfloor x\rfloor\).
Then \(\mathbb E\,q_s=x\), so the identity is the exact derivative of the *expected quantised value*. For a non-linear loss it is not the derivative of the
expected loss. For \(L(q)=(q-t)^2\),

$$
\mathbb E\,L=(x-t)^2+\operatorname{Var}q_s=(x-t)^2+p(1-p),\qquad
\frac{\mathrm d\,\mathbb E L}{\mathrm dx}=2(x-t)+1-2p,
$$
\alttext(The expected loss equals x minus t squared plus the variance of q s, which is p times 1 minus p. Its derivative is 2 times x minus t plus 1 minus 2 p.)

while the STE gradient has mean \(\mathbb E\,2(q_s-t)=2(x-t)\). The bias \(1-2p\) lies in \((-1,1]\). Inside each unit interval \(\mathbb E L\) is linear, because
\((x-t)^2+p(1-p)\) with \(p=x-\lfloor x\rfloor\) has no \(x^2\) term. Its minimum therefore lies at an integer, where rounding is deterministic.
STE descent instead stops at \(x=t\), paying the variance \(p(1-p)\) in expected loss.

>> Gradient reversal, scaling and clipping

Three more custom functions are the identity in the forward pass and change only the gradient. `nn::gradient_reversal(x, λ)`
returns \(-\lambda\bar y\). Placed between a feature extractor \(f_\theta\) and a domain classifier \(d_\psi\) (Ganin and Lempitsky, 2015), it lets one
optimiser step descend the classifier's loss in \(\psi\) while ascending it in \(\theta\), that is, solve
\(\min_\theta\max_\psi\) with ordinary gradient descent. `nn::scale_gradient(x, c)` returns \(c\,\bar y\), which gives per-layer learning rates or stops part of a
network learning (\(c=0\)) without changing its output. `nn::clip_gradient(x, a, b)` clips each element of \(\bar y\) to \([a,b]\) at one point in the graph,
unlike the global norm clipping of the optimiser reports.

> Simple examples

>> A custom gradient for erfc

The program defines erfc with the backward derived above, written with `nn` operations, and differentiates it
twice. It then checks it with `gradcheck` and `gradgradcheck`, and flips the sign of the backward to see that the check
catches the error. A last line shows what happens without any custom gradient. `nn::ceil` records no history, so
a gradient through it is reported as an error, not returned as zero.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using Tensors = std::vector<Tensor>;

int main() {
  const double pi = std::numbers::pi;
  // erfc with a hand-written backward, d/dx erfc(x) = -2/sqrt(pi) exp(-x^2), built from nn ops
  // so that the backward is itself differentiable.
  const nn::Function erfc_fn(
      "Erfc", [](const Tensors& in) { return Tensors{nn::erfc(in[0])}; },
      [pi](const Tensors& in, const Tensors&, const Tensors& g) {
        return Tensors{g[0] * nn::exp(-nn::square(in[0])) * (-2.0 / std::sqrt(pi))};
      });

  const std::vector<double> xs{-1.5, -0.5, 0.0, 0.5, 1.5};
  auto x = nn::from_vector(xs, {5}).requires_grad_();
  const Tensor y  = erfc_fn(x);
  const Tensor g1 = nn::grad(nn::sum(y), {x}, {.create_graph = true})[0];
  const Tensor g2 = nn::grad(nn::sum(g1), {x})[0];
  std::print("| x | erfc(x) | custom gradient | closed form | central difference, h = 1e-5 | second derivative | closed form 4x e^(-x²)/√π |\n");
  std::print("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  const auto gv = g1.to_vector<double>(), g2v = g2.to_vector<double>(), yv = y.to_vector<double>();
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < xs.size(); ++i) {
    const double xi = xs[i], h = 1e-5;
    std::print("| {:.1f} | {:.6f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} |\n", xi, yv[i], gv[i],
               -2 / std::sqrt(pi) * std::exp(-xi * xi), (std::erfc(xi + h) - std::erfc(xi - h)) / (2 * h), g2v[i],
               4 * xi * std::exp(-xi * xi) / std::sqrt(pi));
  }
  const auto r1 = nn::gradcheck([&](const Tensors& in) { return erfc_fn(in[0]); }, {x.detach().clone().requires_grad_()});
  const auto r2 = nn::gradgradcheck([&](const Tensors& in) { return erfc_fn(in[0]); }, {x.detach().clone().requires_grad_()});
  std::print("\n- gradcheck: ok = {}, largest error {:.1e}; gradgradcheck: ok = {}, largest error {:.1e}\n", r1.ok,
             r1.max_abs_error, r2.ok, r2.max_abs_error);

  // A deliberately wrong backward is caught by gradcheck.
  const nn::Function wrong(
      "ErfcWrongSign", [](const Tensors& in) { return Tensors{nn::erfc(in[0])}; },
      [pi](const Tensors& in, const Tensors&, const Tensors& g) {
        return Tensors{g[0] * nn::exp(-nn::square(in[0])) * (2.0 / std::sqrt(pi))};
      });
  const auto bad = nn::gradcheck([&](const Tensors& in) { return wrong(in[0]); }, {x.detach().clone().requires_grad_()});
  std::print("- the same backward with the sign flipped: ok = {}, largest error {:.3f}\n", bad.ok, bad.max_abs_error);
  std::print("  - first failure: {}\n", bad.message);

  // The tape shows the custom node.
  auto z = nn::scalar(0.5, nn::DType::Float64).requires_grad_();
  std::print("\n```\n{}```\n", nn::tape({nn::square(erfc_fn(z))}, {{z, "z"}}).to_text());

  // Ops with no useful derivative record no history: a gradient through them is an error.
  try {
    auto c = nn::tensor({0.5}, {1}, nn::DType::Float64).requires_grad_();
    (void)nn::grad(nn::sum(nn::ceil(c)), {c});
  } catch (const std::exception& e) {
    std::print("\n- gradient through ceil: `{}`\n", e.what());
  }
}
```
